WEBVTT

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:05.898
Warp 5...

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.099
warp 6...

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.108
warp 7.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:11.269
I've reached critical velocity.

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:13.142
Okay, everything looks good
on this end.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.612
Fire up the new engines.

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:15.614
Acknowledged.

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:18.242
Engaging transwarp drive
in four...

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:20.718
three, two...

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.682
Transwarp on line!

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:29.326
Warp 9.2...

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.421
9.3...

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.092
My vector's drifting.

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:36.432
Try to stabilize
your field symmetry.

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:37.902
Got it.

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:39.905
Warp 9.6...

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.839
9.7...

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:45.807
I'm reading a fracture
in the port nacelle pylon.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:48.006
Full power
to structural integrity.

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:49.844
Warp 9.9...

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.942
9.95...

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:53.612
I'm approaching the threshold.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.549
But the nacelle isn't holding.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:58.022
Tie in auxiliary power.

00:00:58.124 --> 00:00:59.989
It's no use.

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:00.993
I'm breaking up!

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:02.494
I'm breaking...

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.302
You're dead.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.650
It's the pylon again.

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:14.285
Every time we get close
to crossing the threshold,

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:17.455
the subspace torque rips
a nacelle off the shuttle.

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:20.762
What about a duranium alloy?

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.267
We could try using
it to reinforce...

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:23.269
I thought about that already.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.270
It's too brittle.

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.430
More coffee?

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.639
Well, you look
like a happy bunch.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:30.973
We've hit a wall.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.308
Oh.
Well, maybe I can help.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:36.474
Great. Do you know anything
about quantum warp theory

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:39.849
or multispectral
subspace engine design?

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:42.514
No, but I'm a quick study.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.524
What are we working on?

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.627
Do you have anything to eat?

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.993
Sure. There's
some Kalavian biscuits

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.895
somewhere in the kitchen.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:58.369
I guess
I'll go find them myself.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:02.268
Fill him in.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.276
Neelix...

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.370
it would take too long to...

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.437
What are you saying?

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:09.573
I'm not smart enough?

00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:12.312
I'll have you know
I did two years

00:04:12.352 --> 00:04:14.616
as an engineer's assistant
aboard a Trabalian freighter.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:16.713
I'm well-versed in warp theory.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:19.247
Okay, okay, we'll tell you.

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:23.353
We're trying to break
the maximum warp barrier.

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:26.160
Nothing in the universe
can go warp 10.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.826
It's a theoretical
impossibility.

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:29.801
In principle, if you were ever

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:33.567
to reach warp 10, you'd be
traveling at infinite velocity.

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.302
Infinite velocity.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:39.937
Got it.
So, tha-that means very fast.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:43.772
It means that you would occupy

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:46.683
every point in the universe
simultaneously.

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:48.983
In theory, you could go
any place in the wink of an eye.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.988
Time and distance
would have no meaning.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.524
If Voyager achieved warp 10,

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:56.323
we could be home in as long
as it takes to push a button.

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:58.262
Wow!

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:00.289
And you're working on this?

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:01.925
We discovered
a new form of dilithium

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:04.232
in the asteroid field
we surveyed last month.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.864
It remains stable
at a much higher warp frequency.

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:08.498
The problem is, every time

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:10.771
we simulate crossing
the transwarp threshold,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.244
the nacelles
get torn off the ship.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:17.840
I remember there was a time
when I lost a warp nacelle

00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:19.784
going through
a dark-matter nebula.

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.878
This is
a very different problem.

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:25.288
I realize that.
I'm just using it as an example.

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.885
As the ship went
through the nebula,

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:30.293
it sent out
a dark-matter bow wave.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.093
Eventually,
so much pressure built up,

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:33.689
it tore the nacelle
from its housing.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:37.066
Now, maybe the same thing
is happening to you.

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:38.536
No. The simulations
don't indicate

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:40.505
any kind of subspace stress
on the nacelles.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:41.564
Wait a minute.

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:44.735
What about the shuttle itself?

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.176
What do you mean?

00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:47.975
Maybe we've been looking
in the wrong place.

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:50.311
What if the nacelles
aren't being torn from the ship?

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:52.716
What if the ship is being torn
from the nacelles?

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:55.619
The hull of the shuttle
is made of tritanium alloy.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:58.089
At the speeds
we're talking about,

00:05:58.124 --> 00:05:59.887
that alloy could depolarize...

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:01.951
And create
a velocity differential.

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:04.087
The fuselage would be traveling

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.189
at a faster rate of speed
than the nacelles.

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:07.722
That means, we just
have to set up

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.735
a depolarization matrix
around the fuselage.

00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:11.236
That's it!

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:15.867
Neelix, you're a genius.

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:21.812
I have no idea
what they just said.

00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:27.282
Warp 9.92.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.379
The pylons are secure.

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:31.253
Everything looks good.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.349
9.97...

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:35.327
eight...

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.261
nine...

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.262
Warp 10.

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:45.065
You've crossed the threshold.

00:06:45.104 --> 00:06:46.230
You've done it!

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:47.967
And there's been no damage
to the nacelles.

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:54.440
When you came to me a month ago
and said you had a way

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.607
to cross
the transwarp threshold,

00:06:56.649 --> 00:07:00.016
I thought it was more
of a fantasy than a theory.

00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:04.587
Congratulations to all of you.

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:07.525
So we can try
a manned test flight?

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.790
What's your flight plan?

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:11.353
I'll bring the shuttle
up to speed.

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:12.830
The second
I cross the threshold,

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:15.265
I'll cut the engines,
drop out of warp and come about.

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:18.395
After that, we'll analyze
the shuttle's sensor logs.

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:20.633
Once we know it's safe
to travel at transwarp,

00:07:20.673 --> 00:07:23.164
we'll try
a more extended flight.

00:07:23.209 --> 00:07:25.200
To be honest,
it's almost frightening.

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:27.235
Up till now,
it's all been theory.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.111
I never thought
it would actually happen.

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:31.708
Are we ready for it?

00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:33.241
In the last couple
of centuries,

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:36.449
we've always managed to use
new technologies wisely.

00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:37.581
I'm confident

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:39.853
this time won't
be any different.

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:44.056
Besides, there's no way to put
the genie back in the bottle.

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:48.726
All we can do now is keep
moving forward... carefully.

00:07:48.768 --> 00:07:50.929
Well, good luck, Mr. Paris.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:54.565
If this works, you'll be joining
an elite group of pilots...

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:59.340
Orville Wright, Neil Armstrong,
Zefram Cochrane...

00:07:59.378 --> 00:08:01.403
and Tom Paris.

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:04.075
I kind of like
the way that sounds.

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:06.016
I thought you might.

00:08:17.162 --> 00:08:18.151
Come in.

00:08:19.497 --> 00:08:20.930
Oh. Captain!

00:08:20.999 --> 00:08:21.931
I, um...

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.092
At ease, Mr. Paris.

00:08:23.134 --> 00:08:24.192
I know it's late.

00:08:27.472 --> 00:08:29.702
Can I... get you anything?

00:08:29.741 --> 00:08:31.208
No. Thank you.

00:08:31.242 --> 00:08:36.043
Now, why do I get the feeling
this isn't just a social call?

00:08:37.115 --> 00:08:40.744
I've just spoken to the Doctor
and it's his opinion

00:08:40.785 --> 00:08:43.447
that we let Ensign Kim
make the test flight.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.023
May I ask why?

00:08:52.497 --> 00:08:54.624
He checked over
your biometric readings

00:08:54.666 --> 00:08:56.327
from the shuttle simulations.

00:08:56.367 --> 00:08:58.927
He says you have
a slight enzymatic imbalance

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:00.062
in your cerebellum.

00:09:00.104 --> 00:09:01.071
So?

00:09:01.105 --> 00:09:02.538
I know it doesn't
sound like much,

00:09:02.574 --> 00:09:05.008
but he can't predict
what'll happen

00:09:05.043 --> 00:09:06.635
when you cross the threshold.

00:09:06.678 --> 00:09:08.339
He believes
there's a small chance

00:09:08.379 --> 00:09:09.778
that you could suffer
a brain hemorrhage

00:09:09.814 --> 00:09:11.611
under the subspace stress.

00:09:11.649 --> 00:09:13.344
How big a chance?

00:09:14.485 --> 00:09:15.474
Two percent.

00:09:16.788 --> 00:09:18.517
Two percent?!

00:09:21.226 --> 00:09:22.921
I'll take that chance.

00:09:22.994 --> 00:09:24.484
There's no reason to.

00:09:24.529 --> 00:09:25.928
Ensign Kim is capable

00:09:25.964 --> 00:09:27.522
of piloting the shuttle
for this mission.

00:09:27.565 --> 00:09:29.055
Yeah, he's capable.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.761
That's not the point.

00:09:30.802 --> 00:09:32.269
What is the point, Mr. Paris?

00:09:32.303 --> 00:09:35.295
Well, it's hard to explain,
but...

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.808
this is my flight.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:39.970
Your flight.

00:09:44.816 --> 00:09:47.080
When I was a boy,

00:09:47.118 --> 00:09:49.780
my father used to tell me
that I was special,

00:09:49.821 --> 00:09:54.087
that one day
I'd do something significant.

00:09:54.125 --> 00:09:57.390
My teachers at school,
all the kids,

00:09:57.428 --> 00:10:00.591
everyone used to say,
"Tom Paris is going

00:10:00.632 --> 00:10:02.725
to do something important
when he grows up."

00:10:07.405 --> 00:10:09.669
Obviously, that didn't happen.

00:10:09.707 --> 00:10:11.538
This isn't about
personal redemption.

00:10:11.576 --> 00:10:13.669
We're talking
about medical risk.

00:10:13.711 --> 00:10:16.077
Your life could be in danger,
and we need you.

00:10:16.114 --> 00:10:18.378
Captain, this is the first time
in ten years

00:10:18.416 --> 00:10:20.907
I feel I have a life to risk.

00:10:23.354 --> 00:10:24.844
You're sure about this.

00:10:24.889 --> 00:10:27.722
Captain, I've never been
so sure about anything.

00:10:29.227 --> 00:10:30.854
Please.

00:10:32.897 --> 00:10:34.558
Please, let me make the flight.

00:10:42.974 --> 00:10:44.839
Good luck, Lieutenant.

00:10:54.152 --> 00:10:55.278
Torres to Bridge.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.481
The prelaunch sequence
is complete,

00:10:57.522 --> 00:10:58.489
Captain.

00:10:58.523 --> 00:10:59.490
Acknowledged.

00:10:59.524 --> 00:11:01.389
Ensign Kim,
depressurize the shuttle bay

00:11:01.426 --> 00:11:02.757
and open the space doors.

00:11:02.794 --> 00:11:03.920
Aye, Captain.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.955
Bridge
to Shuttlecraft Cochrane.

00:11:08.066 --> 00:11:09.693
You're cleared for launch.

00:11:09.734 --> 00:11:10.792
Aye, Captain.

00:11:12.704 --> 00:11:14.695
See you at warp 10.

00:11:22.747 --> 00:11:24.078
Cochrane to Voyager.

00:11:24.115 --> 00:11:26.174
All systems are nominal.

00:11:26.217 --> 00:11:27.548
I'm increasing speed.

00:11:27.585 --> 00:11:30.076
We'll keep up with you
as long as we can.

00:11:31.489 --> 00:11:33.889
Warp 7, warp 8...

00:11:33.925 --> 00:11:36.393
How's his dilithium
matrix holding up?

00:11:36.427 --> 00:11:37.985
There's a slight variance
in the warp field,

00:11:38.062 --> 00:11:39.723
but nothing to worry about.

00:11:39.764 --> 00:11:41.891
Okay. Torres
to Shuttlecraft Cochrane.

00:11:41.933 --> 00:11:44.993
You're clear
for transwarp velocity.

00:11:45.069 --> 00:11:46.559
Acknowledged.

00:11:46.604 --> 00:11:49.698
Engaging transwarp drive
in four...

00:11:49.741 --> 00:11:51.231
three...

00:11:51.275 --> 00:11:52.435
two...

00:11:54.579 --> 00:11:57.343
Warp 9.7...

00:11:57.382 --> 00:11:59.350
9.8...

00:11:59.384 --> 00:12:01.352
9.9...

00:12:01.386 --> 00:12:04.082
He is exceeding
our maximum velocity.

00:12:04.122 --> 00:12:06.181
I am switching
to long-range sensors.

00:12:06.224 --> 00:12:07.919
Warp 9.95!

00:12:07.959 --> 00:12:09.927
He is approaching the threshold.

00:12:09.961 --> 00:12:12.429
Engine output at maximum.

00:12:12.463 --> 00:12:14.363
Velocity...

00:12:17.435 --> 00:12:18.902
warp 10.

00:12:18.936 --> 00:12:19.800
Yes!

00:12:19.837 --> 00:12:21.737
Transwarp engines are stable.

00:12:21.773 --> 00:12:23.934
So are the nacelle pylons.

00:12:23.975 --> 00:12:25.670
I'm going to...

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:27.337
Oh, my God...

00:12:27.378 --> 00:12:28.572
Oh, my...

00:12:28.613 --> 00:12:30.103
Lieutenant, can you hear me?

00:12:31.449 --> 00:12:32.416
Captain, he just...

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:34.577
just disappeared off sensors.

00:12:34.619 --> 00:12:36.246
Increase sensor gain to maximum.

00:12:37.422 --> 00:12:38.946
Nothing.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:40.787
I can't find him.

00:12:40.825 --> 00:12:43.020
He's gone.

00:12:51.636 --> 00:12:53.968
I've done
three full sensor sweeps.

00:12:54.005 --> 00:12:56.235
No sign of the shuttle
within five parsecs.

00:12:56.274 --> 00:12:58.435
Tuvok, could the shuttle
have been destroyed?

00:12:58.476 --> 00:12:59.875
I don't believe so.

00:12:59.911 --> 00:13:02.778
Sensors indicate that he did
cross the warp threshold.

00:13:02.814 --> 00:13:05.840
If that's true, then he could be
anywhere in the universe.

00:13:05.883 --> 00:13:08.943
We'll just have to keep
searching our small corner.

00:13:09.053 --> 00:13:10.111
Run a multispectral sweep.

00:13:10.154 --> 00:13:11.121
I want to see if...

00:13:11.155 --> 00:13:12.122
Captain,

00:13:12.156 --> 00:13:14.147
there's a quantum surge
off the port bow.

00:13:14.192 --> 00:13:17.184
Something is coming
out of subspace.

00:13:21.232 --> 00:13:22.699
Janeway to Paris.

00:13:22.733 --> 00:13:25.600
He's alive,
but his life signs are weak.

00:13:25.636 --> 00:13:27.126
Bridge to Transporter Room 2.

00:13:27.171 --> 00:13:28.968
Beam Mr. Paris
directly to Sick Bay.

00:13:29.006 --> 00:13:30.200
Commander, bring
the shuttle aboard.

00:13:30.241 --> 00:13:31.265
I'll be in Sick Bay.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:34.602
Aye, Captain.

00:13:36.214 --> 00:13:37.977
His life signs are normal.

00:13:38.049 --> 00:13:40.517
A few elevated
serotonin readings

00:13:40.551 --> 00:13:41.882
in the hypothalamus.

00:13:43.888 --> 00:13:46.254
From what I can tell,
he's just...

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:48.087
asleep.

00:13:48.125 --> 00:13:49.387
Can you wake him?

00:13:49.427 --> 00:13:50.758
I don't see why not.

00:13:53.931 --> 00:13:55.421
Wake up, Lieutenant!

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:01.533
Are you all right, Tom?

00:14:02.807 --> 00:14:04.206
I'm back.

00:14:04.242 --> 00:14:06.233
We tracked you until you
crossed the threshold.

00:14:06.277 --> 00:14:08.268
Then you disappeared
from our sensors.

00:14:08.312 --> 00:14:10.212
Do you remember what happened?

00:14:13.584 --> 00:14:15.211
Oh, yeah.

00:14:18.723 --> 00:14:19.747
I was...

00:14:21.559 --> 00:14:23.959
I was staring
at the velocity indicator.

00:14:24.028 --> 00:14:26.258
It said warp 10.

00:14:26.297 --> 00:14:29.095
And then, as I watched it,

00:14:29.133 --> 00:14:31.829
I suddenly realized
that I was...

00:14:31.869 --> 00:14:34.702
watching myself as well.

00:14:34.739 --> 00:14:36.468
I could see the outside
of the shuttle.

00:14:36.507 --> 00:14:38.134
I could see Voyager.

00:14:38.175 --> 00:14:40.109
I could see inside Voyager.

00:14:40.144 --> 00:14:42.977
I could see inside this room.

00:14:43.014 --> 00:14:47.144
For a moment, I was everywhere.

00:14:48.653 --> 00:14:52.521
I mean, everywhere, Captain.

00:14:52.557 --> 00:14:53.990
With the Kazon...

00:14:54.091 --> 00:14:56.685
back home, with the Klingons,
other galaxies...

00:14:56.727 --> 00:14:58.388
it was all there.

00:15:00.197 --> 00:15:02.222
I don't know how else
to explain it.

00:15:02.266 --> 00:15:04.393
It was like...

00:15:05.636 --> 00:15:08.264
Well, no, it wasn't
like anything.

00:15:08.306 --> 00:15:09.466
Well...

00:15:10.908 --> 00:15:13.536
I'm glad you had a good time.

00:15:13.578 --> 00:15:15.773
How did you get back to Voyager?

00:15:18.916 --> 00:15:21.680
I saw that you
were looking for me,

00:15:21.719 --> 00:15:23.448
so I took
the new engines off-line

00:15:23.487 --> 00:15:25.318
and ended up
back where I started.

00:15:27.291 --> 00:15:28.451
But...

00:15:29.493 --> 00:15:32.519
Oh, it's starting to slip away.

00:15:34.031 --> 00:15:36.465
It all was so vivid, and now...

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:37.489
Tom!

00:15:38.636 --> 00:15:40.900
Oh, I'm fine.
How's the shuttle?

00:15:40.938 --> 00:15:43.668
You brought it back
without a scratch.

00:15:43.708 --> 00:15:46.575
The onboard sensors
confirm that you did it.

00:15:46.611 --> 00:15:47.976
You made it to warp 10.

00:15:48.079 --> 00:15:50.240
Congratulations, Mr. Paris.

00:15:50.281 --> 00:15:52.306
You just made the history books.

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:54.215
We should download
the shuttle's

00:15:54.251 --> 00:15:55.809
sensor logs, analyze
the telemetry

00:15:55.853 --> 00:15:58.913
they picked up during the flight
before we make another attempt.

00:15:58.956 --> 00:16:00.321
You're not going anywhere...

00:16:00.358 --> 00:16:02.383
at least not for a few hours.

00:16:02.426 --> 00:16:05.395
I have some tests I'd like
to run on your majesty

00:16:05.429 --> 00:16:07.056
before I release you back

00:16:07.098 --> 00:16:09.299
into the realm
of ordinary humans.

00:16:09.432 --> 00:16:11.923
You may... proceed.

00:16:12.035 --> 00:16:13.502
We'll download the logs.

00:16:13.536 --> 00:16:15.401
I'll let you know
what we find.

00:16:25.315 --> 00:16:26.942
Janeway to Kim.
We're ready.

00:16:26.983 --> 00:16:28.416
Acknowledged.

00:16:28.451 --> 00:16:32.512
I'm downloading the logs into
the Engineering computer core.

00:16:34.123 --> 00:16:36.751
This data describes, literally,

00:16:36.793 --> 00:16:39.523
every cubic centimeter
in this sector.

00:16:39.562 --> 00:16:43.123
It's over five billion
gigiquads of information.

00:16:43.166 --> 00:16:46.761
It would appear that the theory
of infinite velocity is correct.

00:16:46.803 --> 00:16:49.169
It may be possible to occupy
every point in the universe

00:16:49.205 --> 00:16:50.399
simultaneously.

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.840
Then, it's just a matter
of navigation.

00:16:52.875 --> 00:16:54.172
If we could figure out

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:57.668
how to come out of transwarp
at a specific point...

00:16:59.082 --> 00:17:00.947
this could get us home.

00:17:00.983 --> 00:17:02.917
Oh, it could do more than that.

00:17:03.019 --> 00:17:06.011
It could change the very nature
of our existence.

00:17:07.757 --> 00:17:08.746
Think of it.

00:17:08.791 --> 00:17:11.089
There would be nothing
beyond our reach.

00:17:11.127 --> 00:17:15.325
This telemetry is giving us an
invaluable record of the sector.

00:17:15.365 --> 00:17:16.889
We can use it
to make a star chart.

00:17:16.933 --> 00:17:18.230
Transfer the shuttle logs

00:17:18.267 --> 00:17:19.928
to Stellar Cartography
for analysis.

00:17:20.036 --> 00:17:21.367
Right.

00:17:26.676 --> 00:17:28.644
This is a new blend.

00:17:28.678 --> 00:17:31.442
I'm calling it "Paris Delight."

00:17:34.751 --> 00:17:36.150
It's in honor of you.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:38.915
What?

00:17:38.955 --> 00:17:40.718
Oh! Oh, thanks.

00:17:40.757 --> 00:17:42.418
That's very flattering.

00:17:42.458 --> 00:17:44.221
I can tell.

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:49.394
Well, you might as well
get used to it.

00:17:49.432 --> 00:17:50.490
You're a hero now.

00:17:50.533 --> 00:17:52.967
I wish I could say
it was nothing.

00:17:56.606 --> 00:17:58.233
Oh!

00:17:58.274 --> 00:18:00.242
Oh, I wish Neelix
would name something after me

00:18:00.276 --> 00:18:01.573
that tasted a little better.

00:18:02.745 --> 00:18:03.712
It smells okay.

00:18:03.746 --> 00:18:04.713
No. Trust me.

00:18:04.747 --> 00:18:06.908
You're taking your life
into your hands.

00:18:06.949 --> 00:18:09.349
So I think the next step

00:18:09.385 --> 00:18:12.149
is to try to duplicate
the first shuttle flight...

00:18:12.188 --> 00:18:13.416
verify our results.

00:18:13.456 --> 00:18:14.582
I agree.

00:18:14.624 --> 00:18:16.922
We have to confirm
that none of this was a fluke.

00:18:17.026 --> 00:18:19.426
But I do think
that we should increase

00:18:19.462 --> 00:18:21.430
the shuttle's memory core
by at least...

00:18:25.568 --> 00:18:27.399
What?
What's wrong?

00:18:29.639 --> 00:18:32.199
I don't think that coffee
is settling too well.

00:18:32.241 --> 00:18:34.835
You want to go back to Sick Bay?

00:18:34.877 --> 00:18:38.540
No, no. I've seen enough
of the Doctor for one day.

00:18:42.185 --> 00:18:43.618
Oh... on second thought,

00:18:43.653 --> 00:18:44.642
maybe I should...

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:53.085
Torres to Transporter Room 2.
Medical emergency.

00:18:53.129 --> 00:18:55.359
Beam Lieutenant Paris
directly to Sick Bay.

00:18:55.398 --> 00:18:56.956
I can't lock on to him.

00:18:56.999 --> 00:18:58.432
His pattern keeps changing.

00:18:58.468 --> 00:18:59.492
Torres to Sick Bay.

00:18:59.535 --> 00:19:01.969
We need a medical team
in the Mess Hall right away.

00:19:02.905 --> 00:19:04.338
Hang on, Tom.

00:19:17.787 --> 00:19:19.049
Hmm.

00:19:19.088 --> 00:19:21.522
It looks like he's having
an allergic reaction.

00:19:21.557 --> 00:19:23.252
What did he ingest?

00:19:23.292 --> 00:19:25.453
Just a cup of Neelix's coffee.

00:19:25.495 --> 00:19:28.225
It's a miracle he's still alive.

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:36.304
This is strange.

00:19:36.339 --> 00:19:37.670
What is?

00:19:37.707 --> 00:19:40.267
He's definitely having
an allergic reaction...

00:19:40.309 --> 00:19:42.072
to the water in the coffee.

00:19:42.111 --> 00:19:44.443
He's allergic to water?

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:46.539
I don't know
how this is possible, but...

00:19:46.582 --> 00:19:49.551
it appears that his entire
biochemistry is changing.

00:19:49.585 --> 00:19:51.576
His electrolytes
are breaking down,

00:19:51.621 --> 00:19:53.054
interstitial fluids
are congealing.

00:20:08.738 --> 00:20:11.400
The alveoli in his lungs
are mutating.

00:20:13.075 --> 00:20:14.975
He's no long processing oxygen.

00:20:15.011 --> 00:20:16.273
What should we do?

00:20:16.312 --> 00:20:17.609
Stand back.

00:20:23.185 --> 00:20:25.346
Computer, erect
an isolation field

00:20:25.388 --> 00:20:26.946
around the surgical bay.

00:20:28.824 --> 00:20:29.984
Computer, remove

00:20:30.092 --> 00:20:31.423
all the air
from within the force field

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:32.791
and replace it
with 80 percent nitrogen

00:20:32.828 --> 00:20:35.319
and 20 percent acidichloride.

00:20:43.839 --> 00:20:44.931
He can breathe...

00:20:44.974 --> 00:20:46.635
for now.

00:20:46.676 --> 00:20:49.110
What's happening to him?

00:20:49.145 --> 00:20:50.635
I have no idea,

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:52.511
but I assume
it had something to do

00:20:52.548 --> 00:20:54.379
with his experience
on the shuttle.

00:21:00.756 --> 00:21:03.554
His cellular membranes
are deteriorating.

00:21:03.593 --> 00:21:05.356
He's dying.

00:21:07.663 --> 00:21:08.630
I need to know more

00:21:08.664 --> 00:21:10.097
about what happened
on that shuttle

00:21:10.132 --> 00:21:11.258
and I need to know it now.

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:13.598
Radiation levels, unexplained
anomalies... anything.

00:21:32.254 --> 00:21:34.916
You're losing me, aren't you?

00:21:34.957 --> 00:21:37.323
I'm going to die.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:39.794
You're too stubborn to die,
Mr. Paris.

00:21:40.997 --> 00:21:43.932
I want to try high-saturation
radiometric therapy.

00:21:43.966 --> 00:21:46.400
It might slow down
his cellular mutation.

00:21:46.435 --> 00:21:49.131
"Here lies Thomas Eugene Paris

00:21:49.171 --> 00:21:50.638
beloved mutant."

00:21:50.673 --> 00:21:51.640
A fitting epitaph,

00:21:51.674 --> 00:21:53.801
but I don't intend
to let you use it just yet.

00:21:53.843 --> 00:21:55.868
Radiometric emitters
are charged and ready.

00:21:55.911 --> 00:21:58.243
Begin with 45 rads per second.

00:21:59.882 --> 00:22:03.875
Great. Now it'll read
"beloved radioactive mutant."

00:22:03.919 --> 00:22:07.480
No effect. Increase the dosage
to 85 rads per second.

00:22:07.523 --> 00:22:08.854
I'm going to try inhibiting

00:22:08.891 --> 00:22:11.189
the cell mitosis with
a bio-suppressor field.

00:22:11.227 --> 00:22:13.525
A big funeral

00:22:13.562 --> 00:22:17.362
with lots of pretty girls
all crying...

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:18.833
except Torres.

00:22:18.868 --> 00:22:20.961
Torres doesn't cry.

00:22:21.003 --> 00:22:22.527
Do you ever notice that?

00:22:22.571 --> 00:22:24.766
I don't trust people
who don't cry.

00:22:24.807 --> 00:22:27.503
Of course, my father...

00:22:27.543 --> 00:22:30.478
he'd say, "Crying
is a sign of weakness."

00:22:30.513 --> 00:22:32.674
I never believed that.

00:22:32.715 --> 00:22:33.943
Do you cry?

00:22:35.518 --> 00:22:37.213
It's not in my program.

00:22:37.253 --> 00:22:39.346
Shame.

00:22:39.388 --> 00:22:42.152
You know, it's funny.

00:22:42.191 --> 00:22:44.625
What I remember most
about being a kid

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:47.857
are the times
I spent in my room crying.

00:22:47.897 --> 00:22:50.593
I liked my room, though.

00:22:50.633 --> 00:22:52.294
It was quiet in there.

00:22:52.334 --> 00:22:54.359
People'd leave me alone.

00:22:54.403 --> 00:22:57.133
I'd keep the door locked...
read, play games.

00:22:59.241 --> 00:23:01.209
I lost my virginity
in that room.

00:23:01.243 --> 00:23:03.768
Seventeen.

00:23:03.813 --> 00:23:05.610
Parents were away
for the weekend.

00:23:05.648 --> 00:23:08.276
I'll note that
in your medical file.

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:12.944
His lymphatic system
is collapsing.

00:23:12.988 --> 00:23:16.856
Increase dosage to 98 rads
per second.

00:23:16.892 --> 00:23:18.757
Pepperoni!

00:23:18.794 --> 00:23:22.730
God, I'd love a pepperoni pizza
with Kavarian olives right now.

00:23:22.765 --> 00:23:23.754
I'm starving!

00:23:23.799 --> 00:23:26.131
His life signs are critical...
maximum dosage.

00:23:27.369 --> 00:23:29.963
Oh... kiss me.

00:23:30.005 --> 00:23:31.165
What?

00:23:31.207 --> 00:23:33.232
Not you. Her.

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:36.341
She can't breathe the atmosphere
in here.

00:23:36.378 --> 00:23:38.243
Consider it a last request.

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:39.269
Sorry, Tom.

00:23:39.315 --> 00:23:41.510
If we let down the force field,
you'll suffocate.

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:44.542
Oh, what's the difference?

00:23:46.155 --> 00:23:47.884
I'm dying anyway.

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:52.819
Do me a favor.

00:23:52.862 --> 00:23:54.853
When I'm gone...

00:23:56.866 --> 00:23:58.834
call Starfleet Headquarters...

00:23:59.969 --> 00:24:02.563
and tell Dad that I did it.

00:24:04.607 --> 00:24:05.904
Tell him...

00:24:12.515 --> 00:24:15.075
Prepare to activate
the neural stimulator.

00:24:15.117 --> 00:24:16.379
Ready.

00:24:17.787 --> 00:24:19.187
Now.

00:24:21.689 --> 00:24:22.951
Again.

00:24:24.859 --> 00:24:25.553
Again!

00:24:35.636 --> 00:24:38.662
No neural activity.

00:24:38.706 --> 00:24:40.765
We can try to revive him.

00:24:40.808 --> 00:24:43.276
His cell membranes
have degraded.

00:24:43.311 --> 00:24:46.371
There's nothing more
we can do.

00:24:46.414 --> 00:24:47.574
Computer...

00:24:49.483 --> 00:24:53.442
remove the acidichloride gas
from the isolation field...

00:24:55.656 --> 00:24:57.055
and deactivate the field.

00:25:18.479 --> 00:25:21.107
It will be necessary
for us to perform an autopsy

00:25:21.148 --> 00:25:23.639
on the Lieutenant's body
in the morning.

00:25:23.684 --> 00:25:26.812
Until then, I...

00:25:28.656 --> 00:25:30.920
suggest you get some rest.

00:26:42.396 --> 00:26:44.330
You're alive.

00:26:56.544 --> 00:26:59.479
Wha... what's happening?

00:27:07.121 --> 00:27:09.681
All of your internal organs
are functioning again.

00:27:09.724 --> 00:27:12.318
In fact...

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:14.453
you seem to have an extra one.

00:27:14.495 --> 00:27:16.190
What?

00:27:18.399 --> 00:27:19.457
You have...

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:21.434
two hearts.

00:27:36.851 --> 00:27:38.148
I have 30 seconds

00:27:38.185 --> 00:27:40.210
before Security picks up
this transmission.

00:27:40.254 --> 00:27:42.518
I'm sending you
all current information

00:27:42.556 --> 00:27:44.922
on the warp 10 shuttle flight.

00:27:44.959 --> 00:27:46.153
Warp 10?

00:27:46.193 --> 00:27:48.184
That's impossible.

00:27:48.229 --> 00:27:49.491
Not anymore.

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:51.691
Lieutenant Paris crossed
the threshold this morning.

00:27:51.732 --> 00:27:53.393
He survived?

00:27:53.434 --> 00:27:54.401
In a manner of speaking.

00:27:54.435 --> 00:27:55.629
It's all in the file.

00:27:56.937 --> 00:27:59.371
This should prove my worth
to you, Rettik.

00:27:59.407 --> 00:28:00.931
We'll see.

00:28:09.617 --> 00:28:13.519
His body is going through
some sort of mutation.

00:28:13.554 --> 00:28:16.819
His DNA is rewriting itself.

00:28:16.857 --> 00:28:18.916
To what end, I don't know.

00:28:18.959 --> 00:28:20.221
Does this have anything to do

00:28:20.261 --> 00:28:22.058
with the enzymatic
imbalance you found?

00:28:22.096 --> 00:28:23.063
No.

00:28:23.097 --> 00:28:24.564
Can you stop it?

00:28:24.598 --> 00:28:26.623
So far, nothing has worked.

00:28:26.667 --> 00:28:28.066
The mutations are
unlike anything

00:28:28.102 --> 00:28:29.763
in Starfleet medical records.

00:28:29.804 --> 00:28:32.034
His internal organs
are being rearranged.

00:28:32.072 --> 00:28:34.939
Some have atrophied
and been absorbed into his body.

00:28:34.975 --> 00:28:37.170
And there are at least
three others that have appeared

00:28:37.211 --> 00:28:38.872
and have no identifiable
function at all.

00:28:38.913 --> 00:28:40.471
What about his brain?

00:28:40.514 --> 00:28:43.176
There are neuro-electrical
transmutations at work.

00:28:43.217 --> 00:28:47.119
His synaptic patterns are
changing almost every second,

00:28:47.154 --> 00:28:50.146
but he does still
recognize me...

00:28:50.191 --> 00:28:51.249
the ship.

00:28:51.292 --> 00:28:53.089
He knows who he is.

00:28:54.228 --> 00:28:55.957
- Can I talk to him?
- Of course.

00:28:56.030 --> 00:28:58.464
But I feel I should caution you

00:28:58.499 --> 00:29:02.492
that while he still is
Tom Paris,

00:29:02.536 --> 00:29:04.834
he's becoming something
else as well.

00:29:04.872 --> 00:29:08.273
His personality is
erratic, unpredictable.

00:29:08.309 --> 00:29:11.506
There are moments of lucidity
interspersed with almost...

00:29:11.545 --> 00:29:13.536
deranged behavior.

00:29:13.581 --> 00:29:15.412
I'll keep it in mind.

00:29:31.799 --> 00:29:34.529
Pretty disgusting, huh?

00:29:34.568 --> 00:29:36.468
You've looked better.

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:40.766
How do you feel?

00:29:41.809 --> 00:29:44.778
Like a lab experiment
gone wrong.

00:29:44.812 --> 00:29:47.610
Well, we're trying to put
that experiment right.

00:29:47.648 --> 00:29:50.515
The Doctor's working on a way
to restore your DNA to its...

00:29:50.551 --> 00:29:51.745
Why?

00:29:51.785 --> 00:29:53.184
Excuse me?

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:55.552
Why?

00:29:55.589 --> 00:29:58.888
What I'm becoming will probably
be better than who I was.

00:29:58.926 --> 00:30:01.224
Lieutenant,
you know that's not true.

00:30:01.262 --> 00:30:03.457
Oh, yes, it is.

00:30:03.497 --> 00:30:04.930
Admit it.

00:30:04.965 --> 00:30:07.934
Part of you will be glad
to see me gone.

00:30:07.968 --> 00:30:09.959
I'm the Maquis traitor
who sits on your Bridge

00:30:10.037 --> 00:30:12.335
and insults your uniform,
remember?

00:30:12.373 --> 00:30:14.568
We're all concerned
about you, Tom.

00:30:14.608 --> 00:30:15.768
We're here to help you.

00:30:15.809 --> 00:30:17.242
No, you're not.

00:30:17.278 --> 00:30:19.371
You're trying to take this
away from me.

00:30:19.413 --> 00:30:20.573
Take what away from you?

00:30:20.614 --> 00:30:22.343
What I'm becoming.

00:30:23.918 --> 00:30:26.443
How do you know
this isn't good for me?

00:30:26.487 --> 00:30:27.977
How do you know this isn't

00:30:28.088 --> 00:30:30.283
the best thing
that's ever happened to me?

00:30:31.392 --> 00:30:33.155
That's a possibility,

00:30:33.193 --> 00:30:35.354
and then again,
it could kill you.

00:30:35.396 --> 00:30:37.864
We need to find out
what's going on.

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:40.995
You're lying...

00:30:41.035 --> 00:30:42.900
just like him...

00:30:42.937 --> 00:30:44.802
just like everyone around here,

00:30:44.838 --> 00:30:48.831
always lying, always telling
me that I'm doing a good job,

00:30:48.876 --> 00:30:52.107
that you're glad I'm on the
ship, but none of that's true.

00:30:53.981 --> 00:30:56.279
Why can't you just say it?!

00:30:56.317 --> 00:30:58.376
You're jealous that I broke
the transwarp barrier

00:30:58.419 --> 00:30:59.784
and now you're hoping I'll die!

00:31:01.188 --> 00:31:02.815
You're right, Doctor.

00:31:02.856 --> 00:31:06.383
I don't think there's anything
I can do here.

00:31:06.427 --> 00:31:07.826
Wait, Captain.

00:31:07.861 --> 00:31:09.226
I'm sorry.

00:31:11.365 --> 00:31:13.492
Please come back.

00:31:15.636 --> 00:31:17.831
I know you're doing
what you can.

00:31:17.871 --> 00:31:18.860
It's just...

00:31:20.808 --> 00:31:22.776
I'm scared.

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:24.903
I didn't mean
to lash out at you.

00:31:24.945 --> 00:31:27.505
What you're going through
would scare any of us,

00:31:27.548 --> 00:31:29.140
but you have to hang on, Tom.

00:31:29.183 --> 00:31:31.481
We're doing everything we can
to help you.

00:31:33.487 --> 00:31:34.920
I know you are.

00:31:37.291 --> 00:31:39.259
And I know you'll fail!

00:31:47.735 --> 00:31:49.566
You know...

00:31:49.603 --> 00:31:52.902
I used to look up to you,

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:57.172
but now you seem so small,
so insignificant.

00:31:58.245 --> 00:32:00.304
You don't even know what...

00:32:00.347 --> 00:32:02.406
what... what...

00:32:28.508 --> 00:32:30.567
Doctor?

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:34.842
The rate of genetic mutation
has accelerated by 12 percent.

00:32:36.549 --> 00:32:38.414
I want you to run
a nucleogenic scan

00:32:38.451 --> 00:32:41.215
to determine how much of his
original DNA is left intact.

00:32:41.254 --> 00:32:42.221
In the meantime,

00:32:42.255 --> 00:32:44.314
I'm going to see about
treating him with...

00:32:44.357 --> 00:32:45.654
Doctor!

00:32:46.893 --> 00:32:48.258
What is it now, Mr. Paris?

00:32:48.294 --> 00:32:50.990
I need to talk.

00:32:51.097 --> 00:32:52.689
So I've noticed.
Is it urgent?

00:32:52.732 --> 00:32:54.199
I'm rather busy right now.

00:32:54.233 --> 00:32:56.394
Urgent.

00:32:56.436 --> 00:32:58.927
Yes, urgent.

00:33:06.546 --> 00:33:08.605
Let...

00:33:08.648 --> 00:33:10.479
me out of here.

00:33:10.516 --> 00:33:13.076
I'm afraid that's not possible.

00:33:13.119 --> 00:33:15.644
Please...

00:33:15.688 --> 00:33:17.713
I can't stay here.

00:33:17.757 --> 00:33:20.123
I have to get off this ship.

00:33:20.159 --> 00:33:21.990
Leave Voyager? Why?

00:33:24.997 --> 00:33:26.396
I...

00:33:26.432 --> 00:33:28.798
understand.

00:33:28.835 --> 00:33:32.362
It's all so clear now.

00:33:32.405 --> 00:33:33.895
What do you understand?

00:33:33.940 --> 00:33:36.465
The present...

00:33:36.509 --> 00:33:37.806
the past...

00:33:37.844 --> 00:33:40.608
they're both in the future.

00:33:40.646 --> 00:33:43.774
The future is in the past.

00:33:43.816 --> 00:33:45.579
I beg your pardon?

00:33:45.618 --> 00:33:47.586
Listen to me!

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:50.987
I am more...

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:53.058
I'm everything.

00:33:55.528 --> 00:33:56.995
Let me go.

00:33:57.096 --> 00:33:58.723
Go where, Tom?

00:33:58.765 --> 00:34:00.426
I can't...

00:34:04.137 --> 00:34:05.536
Please?

00:34:07.573 --> 00:34:09.438
Please!

00:34:13.146 --> 00:34:15.011
Something tells me
we'd better hurry.

00:34:16.516 --> 00:34:18.780
I believe the answer lies
in forcing his DNA

00:34:18.818 --> 00:34:20.547
to revert
to its original coding.

00:34:20.586 --> 00:34:22.918
Once that occurs,
his body should return

00:34:22.955 --> 00:34:23.922
to its former state.

00:34:23.956 --> 00:34:24.923
How do we do that?

00:34:24.957 --> 00:34:28.051
We destroy all of the new DNA
in his body.

00:34:28.094 --> 00:34:30.961
His cells will have to use the
original coding as a blueprint.

00:34:31.030 --> 00:34:33.191
But the only way
to destroy the mutant DNA

00:34:33.232 --> 00:34:35.496
is with highly focused
anti-proton radiation.

00:34:35.535 --> 00:34:36.900
Anti-protons?

00:34:36.936 --> 00:34:38.164
The only place on this ship

00:34:38.204 --> 00:34:40.365
which generates anti-protons
is the warp core.

00:34:40.406 --> 00:34:42.738
Exactly.
I'd like to place Mr. Paris

00:34:42.775 --> 00:34:44.970
in an isotropic restraint
and then infuse it

00:34:45.044 --> 00:34:48.138
with controlled anti-proton
bursts... a tricky venture,

00:34:48.181 --> 00:34:49.773
but I see no other alternative.

00:34:49.816 --> 00:34:52.250
We'll have to take
the warp core off-line.

00:34:52.285 --> 00:34:54.685
Then I'll need about three
hours to set up an interface.

00:34:54.720 --> 00:34:56.085
In three hours, there won't be

00:34:56.122 --> 00:34:57.680
anything left of Mr. Paris
to save.

00:34:57.723 --> 00:35:00.487
We have to make the attempt
within an hour... at the most.

00:35:00.526 --> 00:35:01.754
Get moving.

00:35:26.319 --> 00:35:28.446
What's your status, Lieutenant?

00:35:28.488 --> 00:35:30.888
The interface
is charged and ready.

00:35:30.923 --> 00:35:33.949
Infuse him with a two-second
anti-proton burst.

00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:35.994
Take the warp engines off-line.

00:35:38.231 --> 00:35:42.167
Bleed off.057 AMUs
of anti-proton radiation.

00:35:42.201 --> 00:35:43.828
Shunt it through the interface.

00:35:43.870 --> 00:35:45.599
Transfer underway.

00:35:55.114 --> 00:35:57.981
Doctor, the mutated DNA
in his liver cells

00:35:58.050 --> 00:35:59.312
has begun to deteriorate,

00:35:59.352 --> 00:36:01.479
but the original coding
isn't taking over.

00:36:01.521 --> 00:36:03.546
I don't understand.
This should be working.

00:36:03.589 --> 00:36:06.319
We'll have to increase
the duration of the bursts.

00:36:06.359 --> 00:36:09.055
Lieutenant, prepare
a five-second burst.

00:36:09.095 --> 00:36:11.655
Also you'll need
to begin depolarizing...

00:36:13.065 --> 00:36:14.794
My God.

00:36:14.834 --> 00:36:15.801
Lieutenant!

00:36:15.835 --> 00:36:16.824
Call Security!

00:36:18.137 --> 00:36:20.230
Lieutenant, what's happening?

00:36:20.273 --> 00:36:21.205
I got it right now!

00:36:24.243 --> 00:36:26.302
Shut it all down.

00:36:31.117 --> 00:36:34.280
This is a level-3
security alert.

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:36.288
All hands report
to duty stations.

00:36:42.728 --> 00:36:44.161
Janeway to Bridge.
Report.

00:36:44.196 --> 00:36:46.391
Paris broke out of confinement
in Engineering.

00:36:46.432 --> 00:36:48.957
He used a phaser
on the port plasma conduit.

00:36:49.035 --> 00:36:50.969
We have power failures
all over the ship.

00:36:51.037 --> 00:36:52.129
Where is he now?

00:36:52.171 --> 00:36:53.661
We're having trouble
tracking him.

00:36:53.706 --> 00:36:56.539
We can't get power
to the internal sensors.

00:36:56.576 --> 00:36:59.136
Tuvok has security teams
searching deck by deck.

00:36:59.178 --> 00:37:01.169
I'm on my way to the Bridge.

00:37:13.626 --> 00:37:15.526
Oh, oh...

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:20.130
Phaser discharge on Deck 6.

00:37:20.166 --> 00:37:21.190
Localize it.

00:37:21.233 --> 00:37:24.100
I'm still having trouble
with internal sensors.

00:37:24.136 --> 00:37:28.505
It came from somewhere between
Sections 21 and 27-Alpha.

00:37:29.909 --> 00:37:32.036
Tuvok doesn't have
any security in that area.

00:37:32.078 --> 00:37:33.807
Chakotay to Tuvok.

00:37:33.846 --> 00:37:35.939
We think Paris may be on Deck 6.

00:37:35.982 --> 00:37:39.748
Deploy security
to Sections 21 through 27-Alpha.

00:37:51.197 --> 00:37:54.928
Commander, someone's
depressurizing Shuttle Bay 2.

00:37:54.967 --> 00:37:55.934
What?!

00:37:55.968 --> 00:37:57.458
There's a launch in progress.

00:37:57.503 --> 00:37:59.198
Tractor beam.

00:37:59.238 --> 00:38:00.603
It's off-line.

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:01.834
Tuvok to Bridge.

00:38:01.874 --> 00:38:03.842
We found the Captain's phaser
on Deck 6,

00:38:03.876 --> 00:38:05.366
but there's no sign of her.

00:38:09.415 --> 00:38:11.406
Maintain a sensor lock
on that shuttle.

00:38:12.284 --> 00:38:13.512
I've got main power back.

00:38:13.552 --> 00:38:15.019
Engage warp engines.

00:38:15.054 --> 00:38:16.112
Follow them.

00:38:16.155 --> 00:38:17.247
Aye, Commander.

00:38:22.995 --> 00:38:25.122
They're approaching warp 9.9.

00:38:25.164 --> 00:38:26.859
Increase speed to match.

00:38:26.899 --> 00:38:30.892
Warning. Nearing
maximum warp velocity.

00:38:30.936 --> 00:38:33.234
Structural collapse is imminent.

00:38:34.440 --> 00:38:35.771
Are we in tractor range?

00:38:35.808 --> 00:38:38.868
No. And they're
still accelerating.

00:38:38.911 --> 00:38:40.105
Warp 9.97.

00:38:41.213 --> 00:38:43.181
Warning. At present speed,

00:38:43.215 --> 00:38:45.979
structural failure
in 45 seconds.

00:38:47.553 --> 00:38:49.714
Reduce speed to warp 9.5.

00:38:49.755 --> 00:38:53.156
Keep a sensor lock on them
as long as you can.

00:38:54.360 --> 00:38:55.884
I've lost them, Commander.

00:38:55.928 --> 00:38:58.055
They've gone to transwarp.

00:39:15.815 --> 00:39:19.911
First Officer's Log,
Stardate 49373.4.

00:39:19.985 --> 00:39:22.749
It's taken us three days
to locate the shuttle.

00:39:22.788 --> 00:39:24.517
It appears to have dropped
out of transwarp

00:39:24.557 --> 00:39:27.082
in an uninhabited star system.

00:39:27.126 --> 00:39:29.686
I've re-examined the data
on Mr. Paris' transformation

00:39:29.729 --> 00:39:32.027
and I think I understand
what's happening to him.

00:39:32.064 --> 00:39:35.522
The mutations
we observed are natural.

00:39:35.568 --> 00:39:36.660
Natural.

00:39:36.702 --> 00:39:38.636
The changes in his DNA
are consistent

00:39:38.671 --> 00:39:39.933
with the evolutionary
development

00:39:40.005 --> 00:39:41.267
of the human genotype

00:39:41.307 --> 00:39:43.400
observed over the past
four million years...

00:39:43.442 --> 00:39:47.572
increased brain capacity,
the loss of vestigial organs...

00:39:47.613 --> 00:39:50.912
Are you saying Lieutenant Paris
is evolving?

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:52.474
That's my theory.

00:39:52.518 --> 00:39:54.076
The only difference
between natural

00:39:54.120 --> 00:39:56.088
evolution and what happened
to Mr. Paris

00:39:56.122 --> 00:39:59.091
is that his changes took place
over a 24-hour period.

00:39:59.125 --> 00:40:01.423
Somehow, traveling
at infinite velocity

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:03.792
accelerated the natural
human evolutionary process

00:40:03.829 --> 00:40:04.796
by millions of years.

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:07.526
It's possible
that Mr. Paris represents

00:40:07.566 --> 00:40:09.966
a future stage
in human development.

00:40:10.035 --> 00:40:12.060
Although I can't say
it's very attractive.

00:40:12.104 --> 00:40:13.765
What do we do about it?

00:40:13.806 --> 00:40:16.206
I think my anti-proton approach
was correct.

00:40:16.242 --> 00:40:17.732
However, I'll need
to intensify the treatment

00:40:17.777 --> 00:40:18.903
to restore his original DNA.

00:40:18.944 --> 00:40:20.144
Bridge to Chakotay.

00:40:20.144 --> 00:40:21.133
Bridge to Chakotay.

00:40:21.178 --> 00:40:22.202
Go ahead.

00:40:22.246 --> 00:40:23.975
I think I found
the shuttlecraft, Commander.

00:40:24.047 --> 00:40:24.979
It's on the fourth planet

00:40:25.048 --> 00:40:26.606
in one of the jungles
near the equator.

00:40:26.650 --> 00:40:29.084
Acknowledged.
Tuvok, have a security team

00:40:29.119 --> 00:40:30.643
meet us in Transporter Room 3.

00:41:40.691 --> 00:41:42.591
There are traces of human DNA.

00:41:42.626 --> 00:41:44.116
It's them.

00:41:44.161 --> 00:41:46.686
But I have to admit...

00:41:46.730 --> 00:41:49.130
I'm not sure which one
is the Captain.

00:41:49.166 --> 00:41:50.997
The female...

00:41:51.068 --> 00:41:52.558
obviously.

00:42:08.719 --> 00:42:11.517
I don't know how I'm going
to enter this into the log.

00:42:11.555 --> 00:42:13.853
I look forward to reading it.

00:42:14.891 --> 00:42:17.758
First Officer's Log,
supplemental.

00:42:17.794 --> 00:42:21.321
We've transported the Captain
and Mr. Paris back to Sick Bay.

00:42:21.365 --> 00:42:23.094
As for their "offspring,"

00:42:23.133 --> 00:42:26.864
I've decided to leave them
in their new habitat.

00:42:26.903 --> 00:42:28.097
I've eradicated all traces

00:42:28.138 --> 00:42:30.231
of the mutant DNA
from your system

00:42:30.274 --> 00:42:32.674
and restored
your original genome.

00:42:32.709 --> 00:42:33.971
Congratulations.

00:42:34.077 --> 00:42:35.772
You're human again.

00:42:35.812 --> 00:42:36.870
Thank you, Doctor.

00:42:36.913 --> 00:42:39.381
Captain, it'll take some time
for your genetic codes

00:42:39.416 --> 00:42:40.508
to stabilize.

00:42:40.550 --> 00:42:41.881
I'd like you
to remain in Sick Bay

00:42:41.918 --> 00:42:42.885
for the next three days...

00:42:42.919 --> 00:42:44.250
just to be safe.

00:42:44.288 --> 00:42:46.483
Excuse me.

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:51.893
Captain, uh...

00:42:55.932 --> 00:42:58.696
I've thought
about having children.

00:42:58.735 --> 00:42:59.702
But I must say

00:42:59.736 --> 00:43:01.636
I never considered
having them with you.

00:43:03.373 --> 00:43:05.102
Captain, I'm sorry.

00:43:05.142 --> 00:43:08.111
I-I don't know
what to say except...

00:43:08.145 --> 00:43:10.670
I don't remember
very much about...

00:43:10.714 --> 00:43:12.944
uh...

00:43:13.016 --> 00:43:14.483
you know.

00:43:14.518 --> 00:43:17.112
What makes you think
it was your idea?

00:43:17.154 --> 00:43:19.782
Sometimes it's
the female of a species

00:43:19.823 --> 00:43:21.950
that initiates mating.

00:43:22.059 --> 00:43:25.119
But apology accepted,
nonetheless.

00:43:25.162 --> 00:43:26.857
You may be interested

00:43:26.897 --> 00:43:30.389
to know I'm putting you in
for a commendation.

00:43:30.434 --> 00:43:32.197
Regardless of the outcome,

00:43:32.235 --> 00:43:35.966
you did make
the first transwarp flight.

00:43:37.307 --> 00:43:39.935
Thank you, Captain.

00:43:39.976 --> 00:43:42.206
Is there something wrong,
Lieutenant?

00:43:42.245 --> 00:43:44.713
I don't know.

00:43:44.748 --> 00:43:47.410
I guess this whole experience

00:43:47.451 --> 00:43:50.215
has left me feeling
a little overwhelmed...

00:43:50.253 --> 00:43:52.778
flying at warp 10...

00:43:52.823 --> 00:43:55.121
evolving into a new life-form,

00:43:55.158 --> 00:43:57.922
mating,
having alien offspring...

00:43:57.961 --> 00:43:59.986
You've broken
more than one record.

00:44:00.097 --> 00:44:01.462
That's for sure.

00:44:01.498 --> 00:44:03.227
Breaking the threshold.

00:44:04.534 --> 00:44:05.694
It was...

00:44:07.237 --> 00:44:09.228
incredible.

00:44:09.272 --> 00:44:12.469
But somehow, it doesn't mean
as much as I thought it would.

00:44:12.509 --> 00:44:13.908
Oh?

00:44:13.944 --> 00:44:17.675
I guess I went into this
looking for a quick fix.

00:44:17.714 --> 00:44:20.148
I thought making history
would change things...

00:44:20.183 --> 00:44:22.344
not just my service record...

00:44:22.386 --> 00:44:24.251
my reputation.

00:44:24.287 --> 00:44:25.686
If I'm not mistaken,

00:44:25.722 --> 00:44:28.623
you've changed
quite a few minds on this ship.

00:44:28.658 --> 00:44:31.957
You've earned a lot of people's
respect and admiration.

00:44:32.028 --> 00:44:34.121
Yeah.

00:44:34.164 --> 00:44:35.791
But I'm starting to realize

00:44:35.832 --> 00:44:38.062
that it's not
other people's opinions

00:44:38.101 --> 00:44:39.728
I should be worried about.

00:44:39.770 --> 00:44:42.102
It's mine.

00:44:42.139 --> 00:44:44.607
It seems, Captain,

00:44:44.641 --> 00:44:47.075
that I still have
a few barriers to break.

00:44:47.110 --> 00:44:50.876
I just hope they're not
theoretical impossibilities.

00:44:50.914 --> 00:44:52.142
Somehow...

00:44:54.251 --> 00:44:56.583
I don't think they will be.
